# CHANGELOG

## 20261125

0.15.0: Report when a formula has been deleted from the tap without notice.

1. ~ bin/tap-audit: + tap_git(), + tap_history?(), + tap_root(), + FORMULA_PATHSPEC, + deletions(), + added_with(), + read_declarations(), + tap_declarations(), + declared(), + unreadable_declaration_findings(), + undeclared?(), + deletion_finding(), + undeclared_deletion_findings(), + unresolved_rename_findings(), + deleted_formula_findings(), + report_deletions().
2. ~ bin/tap-audit: ~ report_summary(), ~ anything_found?(), ~ main(): The deletions are reported, counted and exited upon as the README's findings are.
3. ~ bin/tap-audit: /0.14.2/0.15.0/, and the date.
4. ~ test/tap-audit_test.rb: + require 'json', + commit_tap(). The tap under test is committed, a tap with no history being now a finding of its own.
5. ~ test/tap-audit_test.rb: + describe 'a formula deleted from the tap', twelve examples, run to 103.
6. ~ test/tap-audit_test.rb: /0.8.1/0.9.0/, and the date.
7. ~ README.md: The check is described.
8. ~ TODO: ~ 5: A formula retired with nothing to replace it is reported every run until there is a way to say so.


## 20260909

0.14.2: ~ test/tap-audit_test.rb: the fixtures read the machine's git configuration and were failing upon it.

1. ~ test/tap-audit_test.rb: + GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL and GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM set to File::NULL, so the fixtures are built by a git which reads no configuration at all. The file opens by saying the tap under test is built rather than borrowed, and git was borrowing the machine underneath it.
2. tag.gpgSign is true here, which makes the bare `git tag v1.0.0` the fixtures use fail outright with "no tag message?". No fixture carried a tag, so every check which reads one failed: 23 of the 91 examples, among them the orphan, untagged, tag, pinned version, shadow, interpreter and origin checks.
3. The origin check is why this matters rather than merely being untidy. Its four examples are what say remote_findings works, that being the guard written before the pin sweep for the one fault pinning introduces which no reading of a clone can see, and all four were failing while the sweep was being called ready to run.
4. Red at every commit tested back to 0.14.1. How much further back it goes depends upon when tag.gpgSign was set, which nothing records.
5. The suite now passes under a global configuration setting tag.gpgSign, commit.gpgSign and init.defaultBranch, none of which it survived before. Identity was already passed per commit, which is why commits went on working where tags did not.
6. ~ test/tap-audit_test.rb: /0.8.0/0.8.1/, and the date.
7. ~ bin/tap-audit: /0.14.1/0.14.2/, and the date. Nothing the tool does changes here.


## 20260824

0.14.1: + gem 'switches.rb', which the check added at 0.14.0 would want of any formula.

1. + gem 'switches.rb' to bin/tap-audit. A require names a file, and switches.rb and Seamus Abshere's switches gem both ship lib/switches.rb, so with both installed the require alone does not settle which loads. The gem line does. tap-audit had the same omission it would report of another, which 0.14.0's direct-require reading makes plain.
2. It bites only where the other gem is present, which it is not here.
3. ~ bin/tap-audit: /0.14.0/0.14.1/


0.14.0: Report a gem at two versions only where two formulae each require it.

1. ~ bin/tap-audit: + gem_names_required(), + requires_directly?(): a gem named at two versions is a fault of the tap where both formulae reach it directly. Where one carries it only because something else it requires depends upon it, the versions answer to different things and the difference has a reason: git-boot names activesupport 8.1.3.1 because sym wants it, and git-checkout-new-issue names 7.0.7.2 because it requires active_support itself. Separate kegs, separate load paths, no disagreement to settle.
2. ~ bin/tap-audit: ~ resource_findings(): counts the formulae which require the gem rather than those which declare it, and reports only where more than one does.
3. ~ bin/tap-audit: a require does not spell its gem — active_support/core_ext/string is activesupport, net/ssh is net-ssh, http.rb is required as http — so each require is read as the several names it could be, and require_gem's gem_name: is taken as stated.
4. ~ test/tap-audit_test.rb: + requires: upon the fixture, three examples, run to 91. The one which names only a single direct requirer fails without the narrowing; the other two pass either way and pin the reporting itself.
5. ~ README.md: say that the check wants two formulae which each require it directly.
6. It answers today's one remaining finding: activesupport at 7.0.7.2 and 8.1.3.1 was the only resource divergence left in the tap, and it is not one.


